        ":question/id": "7c937587-dc81-5ee9-aa66-a509a87433e2",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4067",
        ":question/problem": "Find the exact solution of the equation $2^{x+1}=3.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we take $\\log_2$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+1} & =3 \\\\ {log}_{2}\u2061({2}^{x+1}) & ={log}_{2}\u2061(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={log}_{2}\u2061(3) \\\\ x & ={log}_{2}\u2061(3)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\log_2 \\left( 3 \\right) - 1.$"
      },
